('h2', "11.1  Introduction"),
('p', "No diagnostic result is ever produced by one person. Between the clinician's order and the treatment decision stand a nurse, a phlebotomist, a courier, a receptionist, a technologist, a pathologist and a data-entry operator. **A laboratory result is a team product**, and it fails at whichever link is weakest. Similarly, no laboratory improves without someone who sets direction, secures resources, resolves conflict and holds the standard \u2014 that is **leadership**. This chapter therefore closes the syllabus by asking: how do professionals work *together*, and who takes them forward?"),
('p', "Note an important point for your answer: in health care, leadership is **not** the same as holding a senior post. A technologist who stops an unsafe run, who trains a junior properly, or who insists that a critical value be telephoned, is exercising leadership \u2014 this is called **'leadership at every level'** or **followership-leadership**."),

('h2', "11.2  Leadership \u2014 Definition and Concepts"),
