('warn', ["**The examiner's expected conclusion on styles: there is no single best style.** An effective leader **diagnoses the situation** \u2014 the urgency, the competence and commitment of the staff, and the nature of the task \u2014 and **flexibly adapts** the style. Autocratic in an emergency, democratic in routine quality work, delegative with experienced experts, coaching with a new recruit. State this explicitly and you demonstrate understanding rather than memorisation."]),
('h3', "C. Classical Theories of Leadership (mention in a long answer)"),
('b', [
    "**Trait theory (Great Man theory)** \u2014 leaders are born with certain innate traits; criticised because no universal set of traits has ever been established.",
    "**Behavioural theories** \u2014 leadership is a set of learnable *behaviours*, not innate traits; includes the Ohio State studies (**initiating structure** and **consideration**) and the Michigan studies (**production-centred** and **employee-centred**).",
    "**Managerial Grid (Blake & Mouton)** \u2014 plots *concern for production* against *concern for people* on a 9 \u00d7 9 grid: 1,1 **impoverished**; 9,1 **task/authority-compliance**; 1,9 **country club**; 5,5 **middle of the road**; **9,9 team management \u2014 the ideal**.",
    "**Contingency and situational theories** \u2014 Fiedler, Hersey & Blanchard, path\u2013goal theory (House), Vroom\u2013Yetton decision model.",
    "**Transactional\u2013Transformational theory** (Burns, Bass) \u2014 as above.",
    "**Emotional intelligence theory (Goleman)** \u2014 the competencies of **self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy and social skill** distinguish outstanding leaders; Goleman also described six styles: **coercive, authoritative (visionary), affiliative, democratic, pacesetting and coaching**."
]),

