('warn', ["**Three terms examiners like to test \u2014 be able to define them:**",
          "- **Groupthink** (Irving Janis): a mode of thinking in a cohesive group in which the desire for harmony and consensus overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives; symptoms include the illusion of invulnerability, self-censorship, pressure on dissenters and an illusion of unanimity. *Remedy:* encourage dissent, appoint a devil's advocate, invite outside opinion, and let the leader state his view **last**.",
          "- **Social loafing** (Ringelmann effect): the tendency of individuals to exert **less effort** when working in a group than when working alone, because individual contribution is not identifiable. *Remedy:* define individual responsibilities, make contributions visible, keep teams small, set individual as well as team goals.",
          "- **Diffusion of responsibility:** where a task is everybody's, it becomes nobody's. *Remedy:* name a single accountable owner for every task \u2014 this is precisely why a critical-value SOP must specify **who** telephones."]),

('h2', "11.10  Conflict and Its Resolution"),
('def', ("Conflict",
         "A process that begins when one party perceives that another party has **negatively affected, or is about to negatively affect, something that the first party cares about** \u2014 arising from incompatible goals, values, interests, perceptions or resource claims.")),
('b', [
    "**Not all conflict is bad.** **Functional (constructive) conflict** about *ideas and methods* improves decisions and drives improvement. **Dysfunctional (destructive) conflict** about *persons and personalities* damages the team. The leader's job is to encourage the first and eliminate the second.",
    "**Common sources in a laboratory:** disputes over duty rosters, leave and night shifts; blame for rejected samples between ward and laboratory; workload inequity; credit and recognition; scarce equipment or space; unclear role boundaries; personality clashes; poor communication; and perceived favouritism."
]),
('h3', "Five Strategies of Conflict Resolution (Thomas\u2013Kilmann model)"),
('tbl', (["Strategy", "Assertiveness / Cooperativeness", "Description", "When appropriate"],
         [["**Competing / Forcing**", "High assertive, low cooperative \u2014 *win\u2013lose*", "Pursuing one's own position at the other's expense; using authority", "A genuine **emergency**; when patient safety demands an immediate decision; on non-negotiable safety or legal issues"],
          ["**Accommodating / Smoothing**", "Low assertive, high cooperative \u2014 *lose\u2013win*", "Yielding to the other's position to preserve the relationship", "The issue is **trivial** to you but important to the other; to build goodwill; when you are wrong"],
          ["**Avoiding / Withdrawal**", "Low assertive, low cooperative \u2014 *lose\u2013lose*", "Sidestepping, postponing or withdrawing", "The issue is trivial; emotions are too hot and a cooling-off period is needed; someone else can resolve it better. **Never** for a safety issue"],
          ["**Compromising**", "Moderate on both \u2014 *partial win\u2013partial lose*", "Each side gives up something to reach a mutually acceptable middle position", "Both goals are moderately important; time is short; a temporary settlement is needed; parties are of equal power"],
          ["**Collaborating / Problem-solving**", "High assertive, high cooperative \u2014 ***win\u2013win***", "Working jointly to find a solution fully satisfying both parties' underlying interests", "**The ideal approach** where the issue is important, time permits and the relationship must be preserved \u2014 e.g. redesigning the sample-transport system with the ward instead of blaming it"]],
         [3.0, 3.6, 5.4, 5.2],
         "Thomas\u2013Kilmann conflict-resolution strategies")),
('h3', "Steps in Resolving a Conflict"),
('n', [
    "**Acknowledge that the conflict exists** \u2014 ignoring it lets it grow.",
    "**Act early**, and in **private** \u2014 never confront a colleague in front of patients or juniors.",
    "**Gather the facts** from all sides before forming a view; separate fact from perception.",
    "**Attack the problem, not the person** \u2014 use 'I' statements and describe behaviour, not character.",
    "**Listen actively** to each party in full; let them ventilate.",
    "**Identify the underlying interests**, not just the stated positions \u2014 the ward wants reliable results; the laboratory wants valid samples; the shared interest is the patient.",
    "**Find common ground** \u2014 the patient's welfare is always common ground in health care.",
    "**Generate options** together and evaluate them against the shared goal.",
    "**Agree on a specific solution**, with who will do what by when.",
    "**Document** the agreement where appropriate, and communicate it to those affected.",
    "**Follow up** to confirm the solution is working and the relationship has healed.",
    "**Escalate** to a higher authority or a formal grievance mechanism only if resolution fails, or where misconduct, harassment or patient safety is involved."
]),

('h2', "11.11  Leadership and Teamwork in the Laboratory \u2014 Practical Application"),
('tbl', (["Situation", "Leadership / teamwork behaviour expected"],
         [["**A junior mislabels a sample**", "Do not humiliate publicly. Correct the immediate risk to the patient; then teach privately; ask 'what in the system allowed this?'; strengthen the bedside-labelling process; document the incident in a **blame-free** report. **Fix the system, not just the person**"],
          ["**The ward blames the laboratory for a delay**", "Do not counter-blame. Collaborate: map the actual sample journey together, find where the time was lost, agree a joint fix (transport slots, a dedicated STAT window), and report the improvement back to them. This is **collaborating (win\u2013win)**"],
          ["**Night shift is short-staffed**", "Advocate upward with data (workload figures, TAT, error rates), not with complaints; redistribute tasks fairly in the meantime; ensure the person on duty is supported and relieved"],
          ["**Introducing a new analyser or LIS**", "Change management: explain the *why* first; involve the staff early; train thoroughly before go-live; run parallel systems; identify and support the resisters; celebrate the first successful week"],
          ["**A colleague is repeatedly rude to patients**", "Address it privately and specifically; describe the behaviour and its effect; listen for underlying stress or burnout; set a clear expectation; follow up; escalate only if it continues"],
          ["**A senior instructs you to release results despite failed QC**", "Assertive refusal with the reason; offer the alternative (re-run, repeat calibration, inform the clinician of the delay); escalate in writing if pressed. **Moral courage is a leadership quality**"],
          ["**Building a quality culture**", "Lead by example \u2014 wear the PPE, run the QC, document; make incident reporting safe; hold short daily huddles; display the quality indicators publicly; recognise the person who *reports* an error, not only the person who avoids one"],
          ["**Team morale is low**", "Find out why by asking and listening; address fairness in rosters and leave first (perceived unfairness is the biggest morale-killer); recognise good work publicly; create small wins; involve the team in one visible improvement project"]],
         [4.4, 12.8],
         "Leadership and teamwork in action in the laboratory")),

('key', [
    "**Leadership** = influencing and motivating people towards a common goal; elements = leader, followers, goal, influence, situation.",
    "**Leader vs Manager:** leader does the **right things** (vision, change, personal influence); manager does **things right** (systems, stability, positional authority).",
    "Sources of power (**French & Raven**): legitimate, reward, coercive, **expert**, **referent**, informational. Expert and referent power matter most in health care.",
    "**Lewin's three styles:** **Autocratic** (fast, for emergencies), **Democratic** (best for routine \u2014 high morale), **Laissez-faire** (only for expert, self-motivated teams).",
    "Other styles: **transactional, transformational (the four I's \u2014 best in health care), servant, situational (Hersey & Blanchard), contingency (Fiedler), bureaucratic, charismatic, paternalistic, coaching, distributed, authentic**.",
    "**Blake & Mouton's Managerial Grid \u2014 9,9 'team management' is the ideal.** **Goleman** \u2014 emotional intelligence: self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy, social skill.",
    "**No single best style** \u2014 the effective leader diagnoses the situation and adapts. Say this in the conclusion.",
    "**Group vs Team:** a team has one shared goal, complementary skills, high interdependence, **mutual accountability** and **synergy**.",
    "**Tuckman: Forming \u2192 Storming \u2192 Norming \u2192 Performing \u2192 Adjourning**; the leader's role shifts **Direct \u2192 Coach \u2192 Support \u2192 Delegate \u2192 Close**.",
    "**Psychological safety** is the strongest single predictor of team effectiveness \u2014 people must be able to speak up without fear.",
    "Barriers: poor communication and handover, steep hierarchy, silos, blame culture, unclear roles, **groupthink**, **social loafing**, **diffusion of responsibility**, understaffing.",
    "**Thomas\u2013Kilmann conflict styles:** competing, accommodating, avoiding, compromising and **collaborating (win\u2013win \u2014 the ideal)**.",
    "Teamwork is a **patient-safety intervention** \u2014 most serious errors arise from communication and teamwork failure."
]),

