('qa', ("QUESTION BANK \u2014 CHAPTER 11", [
    ("Define leadership. Enumerate the qualities of a good leader.",
     ["Definition as in section 11.2, with the five elements \u2014 leader, followers, shared goal, influence and situation \u2014 and Drucker's remark that a leader is 'someone who has followers'.",
      "Qualities, grouped: **personal** \u2014 integrity, confidence with humility, self-awareness and emotional intelligence, emotional stability, courage, enthusiasm, resilience, discipline; **professional/intellectual** \u2014 technical competence, vision, sound and timely decision-making, problem-solving, planning, knowledge of ethics and law, continuous learning, adaptability; **interpersonal** \u2014 communication, empathy, ability to motivate, team-building, delegation, fairness, conflict resolution, mentoring, approachability, cultural sensitivity; **ethical/organisational** \u2014 accountability, leading by example, advocacy, commitment to quality and safety, transparency, stewardship, good followership. *(Mnemonic: LEADERSHIP.)*"]),
    ("Differentiate between a leader and a manager.",
     ["Reproduce the comparison table \u2014 focus (right things vs things right), basis of authority (personal influence vs formal position), orientation (change vs stability), approach to people (inspires vs directs), question asked (what and why vs how and when), time horizon, attitude to risk, and followers vs subordinates. Conclude that health care needs both and that senior professionals must combine the two roles."]),
    ("Describe the styles of leadership with their advantages and disadvantages.",
     ["**Lewin's three:** **Autocratic** \u2014 leader alone decides; fast and clear, good in an emergency and with untrained staff; but kills initiative and morale and suppresses error reporting. **Democratic/participative** \u2014 consults the team; high morale, ownership and better decisions; but slow and unsuitable in a crisis. **Laissez-faire** \u2014 minimal direction; suits expert self-motivated staff; risks chaos and poor accountability.",
      "Add **transactional** and **transformational** (the four I's \u2014 idealised influence, inspirational motivation, intellectual stimulation, individualised consideration), **servant**, **situational** (Hersey & Blanchard \u2014 directing, coaching, supporting, delegating), **bureaucratic**, **charismatic** and **paternalistic**. Conclude that **there is no single best style**; the effective leader adapts to the urgency, the task and the maturity of the staff."]),
    ("Write short notes on transformational leadership.",
     ["Concept (Burns, developed by Bass): the leader transforms followers by raising their motivation, values and aspirations, so that they achieve more than they believed possible. The **four I's**: **Idealised influence** \u2014 acts as a role model and earns trust; **Inspirational motivation** \u2014 communicates a compelling vision and high expectations; **Intellectual stimulation** \u2014 encourages questioning, creativity and challenge to assumptions; **Individualised consideration** \u2014 mentors and attends to each member's growth. Contrast with **transactional leadership**, which is based on exchange, targets and management by exception. Significance: in health care, transformational leadership is associated with higher staff satisfaction and retention, stronger safety culture and better patient outcomes."]),
    ("Differentiate between a group and a team.",
     ["Reproduce the table \u2014 goal (individual vs one shared), accountability (individual vs individual **and** mutual), skills (random vs complementary), interdependence (low vs high), leadership (single appointed vs often shared), output (sum of parts vs **synergy**), and examples. Emphasise **mutual accountability** and **synergy** as the defining features of a team."]),
    ("Describe Tuckman's stages of team development.",
     ["**Forming** \u2014 polite, anxious, dependent on the leader; goals and roles unclear; leader must **direct**. **Storming** \u2014 conflict over roles, authority and methods; cliques; dip in morale; leader must **coach** and resolve conflict; this stage is normal and necessary. **Norming** \u2014 norms, trust and cohesion develop; roles accepted; leader **supports**. **Performing** \u2014 mature, interdependent, highly productive, self-correcting; leader **delegates**. **Adjourning/Mourning** (Tuckman & Jensen, 1977) \u2014 the team disbands; leader should recognise, celebrate, evaluate, capture the learning and reassign members well. Draw the flow diagram and note that the leader's role shifts **Direct \u2192 Coach \u2192 Support \u2192 Delegate \u2192 Close**."]),
    ("What are the characteristics of an effective health-care team?",
     ["A clear shared goal; clearly defined roles and responsibilities; open, frequent communication with structured handover (SBAR); mutual trust and respect; **psychological safety** \u2014 the strongest predictor of team effectiveness; complementary skills and appropriate skill-mix; shared leadership and participative decisions; mutual accountability; effective conflict management; cohesion and team identity; flexibility with members covering for each other; competent supportive leadership and adequate resources; continuous learning through debriefs and audits; recognition of achievement; and **situational awareness with cross-monitoring** so that members catch each other's errors. *(Mnemonic: TEAMWORK.)*"]),
    ("Enumerate the barriers to effective teamwork and how to overcome them.",
     ["Classify as **communication** (poor handover, jargon, hierarchy silencing juniors, no SBAR), **structural/organisational** (unclear roles, steep hierarchy, departmental silos, staff rotation, understaffing, conflicting targets), **interpersonal** (mistrust, personality clashes, **blame culture**, bullying, favouritism, territorialism), **leadership** (absent, autocratic or unfair leadership, no recognition, shooting the messenger), **individual** (social loafing, ego, resistance to change, burnout, absenteeism) and **group process** (**groupthink**, dominance by one member, **diffusion of responsibility**).",
      "Remedies: structured communication tools and handovers; clear role definition with a named owner for every task; flattening hierarchy and building **psychological safety**; a just, blame-free culture; team training and joint meetings between laboratory and wards; fair rosters and recognition; adequate staffing; encouraging dissent and appointing a devil's advocate against groupthink; and making individual contributions visible to prevent social loafing."]),
    ("What is groupthink? How can it be prevented?",
     ["Groupthink (Irving Janis) is a mode of thinking in a highly cohesive group in which the desire for unanimity and harmony overrides the realistic appraisal of alternatives, leading to poor decisions. Symptoms: illusion of invulnerability; collective rationalisation; belief in the group's inherent morality; stereotyping of outsiders; direct pressure on dissenters; **self-censorship**; illusion of unanimity; and self-appointed 'mindguards'. Prevention: the leader should state his own view **last** and encourage open criticism; appoint a **devil's advocate**; invite outside experts; break into independent sub-groups; take an anonymous poll; conduct a 'second-chance' meeting before finalising; and reward those who raise objections. Laboratory relevance: a team that unanimously agrees to 'release results despite failed QC because it is always fine' is exhibiting groupthink."]),
    ("Describe the strategies of conflict resolution.",
     ["**Thomas\u2013Kilmann's five strategies**, along the axes of assertiveness and cooperativeness: **Competing/forcing** (win\u2013lose) \u2014 for emergencies and non-negotiable safety issues; **Accommodating** (lose\u2013win) \u2014 when the issue is trivial to you and important to the other, or when you are wrong; **Avoiding** (lose\u2013lose) \u2014 for trivial issues or a cooling-off period, never for a safety issue; **Compromising** \u2014 when both goals are moderately important and time is short; **Collaborating/problem-solving** (**win\u2013win**) \u2014 the ideal, when the issue matters, time permits and the relationship must be preserved.",
      "Add the steps: acknowledge the conflict; act early and in private; gather facts from all sides; attack the problem not the person; listen actively; identify underlying interests rather than positions; find common ground (the patient's welfare); generate and evaluate options; agree a specific solution with owners and deadlines; document; follow up; and escalate only if resolution fails or misconduct or patient safety is involved. Note that **functional conflict about ideas is useful**, while dysfunctional conflict about persons is destructive."]),
    ("How do leadership and teamwork contribute to patient safety?",
     ["The great majority of serious adverse events in health care are traced to failures of **communication, handover and teamwork** rather than to lack of technical knowledge. Effective teams practise cross-monitoring and catch errors before they reach the patient; structured communication (SBAR, read-back, checklists, huddles) prevents mis-transmission of critical information; **psychological safety** ensures that a junior will speak up about a wrong label or an unsafe instruction; a **just, blame-free culture** produces incident reports, which are the raw material of system improvement; and clear allocation of responsibility prevents **diffusion of responsibility** so that the critical value is actually telephoned. Leaders create these conditions \u2014 by role modelling, by resourcing, by fairness and by insisting on quality. Hence leadership and teamwork are not 'soft skills' but **direct determinants of whether patients are harmed**."]),
])),

